---
// /mls-photo-requirements/bay-east/ — Bay East MLS Rules 11.5.1 / 11.5.2 (effective 2026-01-01).
// Source quotes: docs/mls-sources.md § Bay East (primary-source check 2026-11-08).
// The source page doesn't name the MLS, so this page doesn't either.
import ArticleLayout from "../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro";

const path = "/mls-photo-requirements/bay-east/";
const SOURCE = "https://bayeast.org/digitally-altered-mls-photo-rule/";
const title = "Bay East MLS Rule 11.5.2: Digitally Altered Photos";
const h1 = "Bay East MLS rules for digitally altered photos (11.5.1 and 11.5.2)";
const description =
  "Bay East's MLS rule for altered photos: upload the original, place it immediately after, and label the edit. Which labels count and what fails.";

const faq = [
  {
    q: "Can the original go before the altered photo on Bay East?",
    a: "The rule says immediately after. Rule 11.5.2 requires the original, unaltered version to be displayed immediately after the digitally altered image. CRMLS allows before or after; Bay East's text doesn't.",
  },
  {
    q: "Can I label a staged photo \"Virtually Staged\"?",
    a: "Bay East's FAQ gives \"Virtually Staged\" as an example label, but the rule text lists \"altered\", \"digitally altered\" and \"AI altered\" and has no \"substantially similar\" clause. \"Virtually Staged – Digitally Altered\" satisfies both readings.",
  },
  {
    q: "Is the photographer or the agent responsible?",
    a: "Bay East says the listing agent and broker are responsible for making sure listing content complies with MLS rules, regardless of who created or edited the photos.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do listings entered before January 1, 2026 need fixing?",
    a: "Bay East encourages agents to review Active, Pending and Hold listings for compliance. Labels can be added or updated at any time.",
  },
];

const schema = [
  {
    "@type": "Article",
    headline: h1,
    description,
    mainEntityOfPage: `https://retouchlint.com${path}`,
    author: { "@type": "Organization", name: "RetouchLint" },
    publisher: { "@id": "https://retouchlint.com/#organization" },
  },
  {
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faq.map((f) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: f.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: f.a },
    })),
  },
];

const related = [
  { url: "/mls-photo-requirements/", anchor: "All California MLS photo rules compared" },
  { url: "/mls-photo-requirements/crmls/", anchor: "CRMLS Rule 11.5.2 and its banned edits" },
  { url: "/mls-photo-requirements/mlslistings/", anchor: "MLSListings Rule 7.11.2" },
  { url: "/blog/what-counts-as-material-alteration/", anchor: "Which edits count as an alteration" },
];
---
<ArticleLayout
  title={title}
  h1={h1}
  description={description}
  path={path}
  related={related}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { name: "Home", url: "/" },
    { name: "MLS photo requirements", url: "/mls-photo-requirements/" },
    { name: "Bay East", url: path },
  ]}
  schema={schema}
>
  <p>
    Under the MLS rules Bay East published for January 1, 2026, a digitally altered listing
    photo needs its original, unaltered version uploaded and displayed immediately after
    it. The altered photo must be marked in a reasonably conspicuous way as "altered",
    "digitally altered" or "AI altered". What counts as altered follows AB 723 word for
    word.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 11.5.1: what counts as digitally altered</h2>
  <p>
    The definition matches Business and Professions Code § 10140.8(b). An image is
    digitally altered when photo-editing software or AI adds, removes or changes elements.
    The list includes fixtures, furniture, appliances, flooring, walls, paint color,
    hardscape, landscape, façade and floor plans, plus things outside or visible from the
    property such as streetlights, utility poles, views through windows and neighboring
    properties. Edits limited to lighting, sharpening, white balance, color correction,
    angle, straightening, cropping or exposure are not alterations.
  </p>
  <p>
    So unlike CRMLS, Bay East doesn't ban landscape or utility-pole edits. They are
    alterations that need the original and the label.
  </p>

  <h2>Rule 11.5.2: what you have to do</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Submit the original, unaltered version of the altered image.</li>
    <li>Display the original immediately after the altered image.</li>
    <li>Mark the altered image "altered", "digitally altered" or "AI altered", reasonably conspicuously.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Bay East's instructions: after uploading, click "Label" beneath the photo, enter the
    label text and save. The label then shows on the listing.
  </p>

  <h2>The label wording question</h2>
  <p>
    Bay East's FAQ offers "Digitally Altered Image", "Virtually Staged" and "Digital
    Rendering" as example labels, and says the label must clearly disclose that the image
    has been digitally altered. The rule text itself lists only the three terms above. A
    label that contains "Digitally Altered" meets both, for example "Virtually Staged –
    Digitally Altered".
  </p>

  <h2>Worked examples</h2>
  <h3>Staged living room</h3>
  <p>
    Upload the staged photo labeled "Virtually Staged – Digitally Altered" and put the
    empty-room original directly after it. The same order also works on CRMLS, which
    accepts before or after.
  </p>
  <h3>Sky replaced and wires removed on the front exterior</h3>
  <p>
    Bay East's FAQ names sky replacement as an alteration, and utility poles are on the
    rule's list, so it's an altered image: label it and put the original right after it. If the listing is also on CRMLS, the
    wire removal is not allowed there at all. See the <a href="/mls-photo-requirements/crmls/">CRMLS
    rule</a>.
  </p>
  <h3>Original uploaded at the end of the gallery: fails</h3>
  <p>
    The original is in the listing, but not immediately after the altered photo. Drag it
    into place. For advertising outside the MLS, add the AB 723 statement with a link to
    the original; the <a href="/tools/disclosure-generator/">disclosure generator</a>
    writes it.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions</h2>
  {faq.map((f) => (
    <>
      <h3>{f.q}</h3>
      <p>{f.a}</p>
    </>
  ))}
  <p class="text-sm">
    Source: <a href={SOURCE}>Bay East Association of REALTORS®, "Digitally Altered MLS
    Photo Rule"</a>. Check the current rules before relying on this summary; this is not
    legal advice.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
